;; Tröskel — a fictional company used to fill a simmis workspace with something
;; worth looking at, and to give agent benchmarks a world to reason over.
;;
;; The premise is deliberately agent-shaped: property management is a stream of
;; small judgement calls against written policy (is this repair urgent? does this
;; deposit deduction comply? has rent actually cleared?), which is exactly the
;; work an agent can do if — and only if — it can read the handbook, the ledger
;; and the conversation together. That is the product claim, so the demo data
;; has to make the claim checkable rather than assert it.
;;
;; Everything here is invented. Any resemblance to a real company is accidental.
;;
;; Timestamps are explicit so the seeded timeline is identical on every run;
;; see is.simm.demo.scenario for why that matters.

{:company "Tröskel"
 :tagline "Agentic property management"

 :kbs
 [{:name "Tröskel Handbook"
   :pages
   [{:title "Tröskel"
     :at "2026-03-02T09:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p><strong>Tröskel</strong> builds agentic software for residential letting agents. Our customers manage between 200 and 4,000 units and are drowning in judgement calls that are individually small and collectively enormous.</p>"
      "<p>The wedge is <strong>maintenance triage</strong>: an agent reads the tenant's message, checks the lease, checks the [[Repair Priority Matrix]], and either books a contractor or escalates. Everything else we do grows from earning trust on that one loop.</p>"
      "<p>Start here: [[How We Work]] · [[Product Principles]] · [[Repair Priority Matrix]] · [[Escalation Policy]]</p>"
      "<p><strong>Stage:</strong> seed · <strong>Team:</strong> 11 · <strong>HQ:</strong> Gothenburg</p>"]}

    {:title "How We Work"
     :at "2026-03-02T10:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>Three habits, in priority order.</p>"
      "<p><strong>1. Write the policy down before automating it.</strong> An agent that follows an unwritten rule is guessing. If a decision cannot be stated as a page in this handbook, it is not ready to be delegated — see [[Repair Priority Matrix]] for the shape we aim at.</p>"
      "<p><strong>2. Propose, then merge.</strong> Agents do not write to shared knowledge directly. They open a proposal; a human accepts it. See [[Proposal Review]].</p>"
      "<p><strong>3. Escalate early and cheaply.</strong> A wrong escalation costs a minute. A wrong autonomous decision costs a tenancy. [[Escalation Policy]].</p>"]}

    {:title "Product Principles"
     :at "2026-03-03T09:30:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p><strong>The agent must be able to show its work.</strong> Every automated decision links to the policy page it applied and the ledger entry it read. If we cannot explain a decision to a landlord, we do not ship it.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Never act on money without a human.</strong> Deposit deductions, rent adjustments and contractor payments over the threshold in [[Spend Authority]] always route to a person.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Prefer boring, checkable behaviour.</strong> A predictable agent that escalates too often beats a clever one that surprises. See [[Escalation Policy]].</p>"]}

    {:title "Repair Priority Matrix"
     :at "2026-03-04T11:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>The core triage table. Agents apply this before booking any contractor; anything not covered escalates under [[Escalation Policy]].</p>"
      "<p><strong>P1 — same day.</strong> No heat below 5°C outside, no hot water, no working toilet in a single-bathroom unit, water ingress, gas smell, failed lock on an external door, anything the tenant describes as unsafe.</p>"
      "<p><strong>P2 — within 3 working days.</strong> Single appliance failure, intermittent heating, minor leak contained by a bucket, one of several bathrooms out of service.</p>"
      "<p><strong>P3 — within 20 working days.</strong> Cosmetic damage, slow drainage, worn fittings, non-urgent decorating.</p>"
      "<p>Gas smell and electrical burning smell are <strong>never</strong> P2 regardless of tenant tone. See [[Gas and Electrical Incidents]].</p>"
      "<p>Cost thresholds are in [[Spend Authority]]. Contractor selection is in [[Contractor Panel]].</p>"]}

    {:title "Gas and Electrical Incidents"
     :at "2026-03-04T11:30:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>Absolute rules. No agent discretion, no exceptions, no cost threshold.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Reported gas smell:</strong> instruct the tenant to leave the property, not to touch switches, and to call the national gas emergency line. Then notify the landlord and log the incident. Book a Gas Safe engineer only after the emergency service has attended.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Burning smell or visible arcing:</strong> instruct the tenant to isolate at the consumer unit if safe, otherwise leave. Book a qualified electrician same-day under P1 in [[Repair Priority Matrix]].</p>"
      "<p>Both cases are automatically reportable under [[Compliance Calendar]].</p>"]}

    {:title "Escalation Policy"
     :at "2026-03-05T09:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>Escalate to a human whenever any of the following is true.</p>"
      "<p>The tenant is distressed, threatening legal action, or mentions a vulnerable occupant (child under 5, elderly, disabled, pregnant).</p>"
      "<p>Money is involved beyond the agent's authority in [[Spend Authority]].</p>"
      "<p>The lease says something the handbook does not cover, or the two disagree — see [[Lease Clause Library]].</p>"
      "<p>The same issue has been reported twice in 30 days. A repeat is a signal the first fix failed.</p>"
      "<p>Escalation is not failure. [[Product Principles]] treats an over-escalating agent as correct and a surprising one as broken.</p>"]}

    {:title "Spend Authority"
     :at "2026-03-05T10:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>What an agent may commit without a human, per incident.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Up to 2 000 SEK</strong> — book a panel contractor from [[Contractor Panel]] without approval, provided the work is P1 or P2 in [[Repair Priority Matrix]].</p>"
      "<p><strong>2 000 – 10 000 SEK</strong> — needs the property manager's approval in the room. The agent prepares the quote comparison; a human presses the button.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Above 10 000 SEK</strong> — needs landlord approval in writing, and the request must state the consequence of not doing the work.</p>"
      "<p>Deposit deductions are never automatic at any value. See [[Deposit Returns]].</p>"]}

    {:title "Contractor Panel"
     :at "2026-03-06T09:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>Approved contractors, by trade and region. Agents book from this list only; anyone else needs approval under [[Spend Authority]].</p>"
      "<p><strong>Plumbing — Göteborg:</strong> Nordvik Rör (P1 capable, 2h response), Lindqvist VVS (P2/P3, cheaper).</p>"
      "<p><strong>Electrical — Göteborg:</strong> Sahlin El (Gas Safe equivalent certified, P1), Öberg Elektriska (P2/P3).</p>"
      "<p><strong>Heating — all regions:</strong> Värmepartner AB, contracted SLA of 8 hours for total heat loss.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Locks — all regions:</strong> Låsteam Väst, 24h call-out.</p>"
      "<p>Panel review is quarterly; see [[Compliance Calendar]]. Removal criteria are in [[Contractor Performance]].</p>"]}

    {:title "Contractor Performance"
     :at "2026-03-06T10:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>A contractor leaves the panel in [[Contractor Panel]] if any of the following holds over a rolling quarter.</p>"
      "<p>More than 10% of jobs require a return visit for the same fault.</p>"
      "<p>Median response time exceeds the agreed SLA by more than 50%.</p>"
      "<p>Any safety finding, or any invoice materially above the accepted quote without prior written agreement.</p>"
      "<p>Two or more tenant complaints about conduct.</p>"]}

    {:title "Lease Clause Library"
     :at "2026-03-09T09:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>Standard clauses and what they mean operationally. When a lease and the handbook disagree, <strong>the lease wins</strong> and the case escalates under [[Escalation Policy]].</p>"
      "<p><strong>Repair responsibility:</strong> landlord holds structure, installations, heating and hot water. Tenant holds their own appliances, light bulbs, and damage beyond fair wear.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Access:</strong> 24 hours' written notice except in an emergency as defined in [[Repair Priority Matrix]] P1.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Pets:</strong> written consent required, not unreasonably withheld; consent is per animal, not blanket.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Subletting:</strong> prohibited without consent. Suspected subletting escalates immediately — never handled by an agent.</p>"]}

    {:title "Deposit Returns"
     :at "2026-03-09T11:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>Deposits are the single largest source of disputes, so the process is deliberately slow and human.</p>"
      "<p>Agents may prepare a deduction proposal: line items, evidence from the check-out report, and the matching clause from [[Lease Clause Library]]. They may not send it.</p>"
      "<p>A deduction must distinguish <strong>damage</strong> from <strong>fair wear and tear</strong>. Age of the item matters: a 7-year-old carpet cannot be charged at replacement cost.</p>"
      "<p>Disputed deductions go to the deposit scheme. Our internal target is that fewer than 5% of returns reach dispute — tracked in [[Operating Metrics]].</p>"]}

    {:title "Compliance Calendar"
     :at "2026-03-10T09:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>Recurring statutory obligations. Missing one is the failure mode that ends a letting agency, so these are scheduled rather than remembered.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Annual:</strong> gas safety inspection per property, boiler service, smoke alarm test at tenancy start and annually.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Five-yearly:</strong> electrical installation condition report (EICR).</p>"
      "<p><strong>Ten-yearly:</strong> energy performance certificate.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Per tenancy:</strong> deposit protected within statutory window, inventory completed before move-in, right-to-rent check recorded.</p>"
      "<p>Incidents under [[Gas and Electrical Incidents]] are reportable regardless of schedule.</p>"]}

    {:title "Proposal Review"
     :at "2026-03-11T09:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>Agents never write to this handbook directly. They open a <strong>proposal</strong> — a branch of the knowledge base — and a human accepts or dismisses it.</p>"
      "<p>The reviewer sees a summary of what changed and can expand into the exact blocks added, edited or removed. Trunk is untouched until Accept.</p>"
      "<p>When you dismiss, say why. The reason trains the reviewing agent on what this company considers a good change, and it cannot be reconstructed later.</p>"
      "<p>Related: [[How We Work]], [[Product Principles]].</p>"]}

    {:title "Operating Metrics"
     :at "2026-03-12T09:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>What we watch weekly. Definitions matter more than the numbers — an unstable definition makes a trend meaningless.</p>"
      {:html "<p>Maintenance tickets resolved without human involvement, by priority tier.</p>"
       :viz {:$schema "<p>https://vega.github.io/schema/vega-lite/v5.json</p>"
             :description "Autonomous resolution rate by priority"
             :data {:values [{:tier "P1" :autonomous 12 :escalated 88}
                             {:tier "P2" :autonomous 61 :escalated 39}
                             {:tier "P3" :autonomous 83 :escalated 17}]}
             :transform [{:fold ["autonomous" "escalated"]}]
             :mark "bar"
             :encoding {:x {:field "tier" :type "nominal" :axis {:labelAngle 0}}
                        :y {:field "value" :type "quantitative" :stack "normalize"
                            :axis {:title "share of tickets"}}
                        :color {:field "key" :type "nominal" :title "handling"}}}}
      "<p><strong>Time to first response</strong> — tenant message to any substantive reply. Target under 15 minutes at all hours.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Repeat rate</strong> — same fault reported again within 30 days. Feeds [[Contractor Performance]].</p>"
      "<p><strong>Deposit dispute rate</strong> — see [[Deposit Returns]]. Target under 5%.</p>"]}

    {:title "Onboarding a New Agency"
     :at "2026-03-13T09:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>The first two weeks decide whether a customer stays, because the agent is only as good as the policy it was given.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Week 1:</strong> import the portfolio, map their existing priority tiers onto [[Repair Priority Matrix]], and record every place they disagree with our defaults. Disagreements are the valuable part.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Week 2:</strong> shadow mode — the agent drafts every response but sends nothing. The property manager reviews and corrects. Corrections become proposals under [[Proposal Review]].</p>"
      "<p>Go-live is when the manager stops correcting P3 drafts, not when the calendar says so.</p>"]}

    {:title "Support Playbook"
     :at "2026-03-16T09:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>How we answer customers when the agent got something wrong.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Acknowledge the specific decision</strong>, not the category. \"The agent booked a P2 for your boiler when it should have been P1\" beats \"sorry about the issue\".</p>"
      "<p><strong>Show the policy it applied.</strong> Every automated decision links to a handbook page. If the page was wrong, fix the page and say so — that is a proposal, see [[Proposal Review]].</p>"
      "<p><strong>Never blame the model.</strong> The model is our choice. The policy is our responsibility.</p>"]}

    {:title "Pricing"
     :at "2026-03-17T09:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>Per unit under management, billed monthly, no setup fee.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Core</strong> — 29 SEK/unit/month. Maintenance triage, tenant messaging, [[Compliance Calendar]] tracking.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Full</strong> — 49 SEK/unit/month. Adds rent reconciliation, [[Deposit Returns]] preparation, and landlord reporting.</p>"
      "<p>Below 200 units we quote a flat 6 000 SEK/month — smaller portfolios cost us the same to onboard.</p>"
      "<p>We do not charge per message or per resolved ticket. Charging per agent action rewards a chatty agent, which is the opposite of [[Product Principles]].</p>"]}

    {:title "Competitive Landscape"
     :at "2026-03-18T09:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>Three kinds of competitor, and the honest read on each.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Incumbent property management suites.</strong> Own the system of record and the customer relationship. Slow to ship anything agentic. We win on the triage loop and lose on breadth — so we integrate rather than replace.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Generic AI support tools.</strong> Fast, cheap, and unaware of the lease. They cannot apply [[Repair Priority Matrix]] because they cannot read the tenancy. We win wherever a decision depends on the specific agreement.</p>"
      "<p><strong>In-house scripts.</strong> Larger agencies build their own. We win on the audit trail and the compliance calendar; we lose when the agency has strong engineering and unusual processes.</p>"]}

    {:title "Security and Data"
     :at "2026-03-19T09:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>We hold tenancy agreements, contact details, and payment history. That is regulated personal data and we treat it as such.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Tenant data never leaves the customer's workspace.</strong> Cross-customer learning happens on policy pages, never on tenant records.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Every automated action is attributable</strong> — which agent, which policy version, which ledger entry. See [[Product Principles]].</p>"
      "<p><strong>Retention:</strong> tenancy records for the statutory period after the tenancy ends, messages for 24 months, then deletion.</p>"]}

    {:title "Glossary"
     :at "2026-03-20T09:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p><strong>Unit</strong> — one lettable dwelling. Our billing unit, see [[Pricing]].</p>"
      "<p><strong>Portfolio</strong> — all units under one agency.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Check-out report</strong> — the condition record at tenancy end; the evidence base for [[Deposit Returns]].</p>"
      "<p><strong>Shadow mode</strong> — the agent drafts but does not send. See [[Onboarding a New Agency]].</p>"
      "<p><strong>Panel</strong> — the approved contractor list, [[Contractor Panel]].</p>"]}]}

  {:name "Operations Playbook"
   :pages
   [{:title "Operations Playbook"
     :at "2026-04-01T09:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>Runbooks for the recurring operational situations. The handbook says what our policy is; this says what to actually do, step by step.</p>"
      "<p>[[Tenant Reports No Heat]] · [[Tenant Reports a Leak]] · [[Rent Has Not Cleared]] · [[Move-Out Checklist]] · [[Suspected Subletting]]</p>"]}

    {:title "Tenant Reports No Heat"
     :at "2026-04-01T10:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>The single most common P1. Most cases are not a broken boiler.</p>"
      "<p><strong>1.</strong> Ask three questions before booking anything: is there hot water, is the thermostat calling for heat, and is the boiler showing a fault code.</p>"
      "<p><strong>2.</strong> Hot water present but no heating usually means a thermostat or a diverter valve, which is P2 unless the outside temperature is below 5°C.</p>"
      "<p><strong>3.</strong> No heat and no hot water below 5°C outside is P1 under [[Repair Priority Matrix]]. Book Värmepartner AB from [[Contractor Panel]] and offer temporary heaters.</p>"
      "<p><strong>4.</strong> If the occupant is elderly, very young or unwell, escalate regardless of tier — [[Escalation Policy]].</p>"]}

    {:title "Tenant Reports a Leak"
     :at "2026-04-02T09:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>Water damage compounds, so the priority is containment before diagnosis.</p>"
      "<p><strong>1.</strong> Establish whether the water is clean, grey or from the ceiling. Ceiling water means the flat above is involved and both tenancies are affected.</p>"
      "<p><strong>2.</strong> Tell the tenant where their stopcock is. If we do not know, that is a gap in the property record and should be recorded.</p>"
      "<p><strong>3.</strong> Contained and clean is P2. Uncontained, or any ceiling ingress, or anything near a consumer unit, is P1 — and near electrics also triggers [[Gas and Electrical Incidents]].</p>"
      "<p><strong>4.</strong> Photograph before drying. Insurance claims fail without a before.</p>"]}

    {:title "Rent Has Not Cleared"
     :at "2026-04-03T09:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>Arrears handling is where an over-eager agent does real harm. Tone is the whole job.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Day 1–2:</strong> do nothing. Bank timing accounts for most apparent misses. Check the ledger before believing the calendar.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Day 3:</strong> a single neutral message asking whether the payment was sent. No mention of consequences.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Day 7:</strong> a human contacts the tenant. Never an agent — by this point the reason usually matters more than the amount.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Never</strong> reference eviction, credit reporting or debt collection in an automated message under any circumstances.</p>"]}

    {:title "Move-Out Checklist"
     :at "2026-04-06T09:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>Everything here exists to make [[Deposit Returns]] defensible.</p>"
      "<p>Confirm the notice was valid and record the agreed end date.</p>"
      "<p>Book the check-out inspection within 48 hours of the end date, while the property is still empty and undecorated.</p>"
      "<p>Photograph every room against the move-in inventory, at the same angles. Mismatched angles lose disputes.</p>"
      "<p>Take meter readings and final readings for utilities; record the forwarding address.</p>"
      "<p>Prepare, but do not send, the deduction proposal. A human sends it.</p>"]}

    {:title "Suspected Subletting"
     :at "2026-04-07T09:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>Never handled by an agent. This runbook exists so the agent recognises it and stops.</p>"
      "<p>Signals: post for unknown names, the tenant unreachable while the property is clearly occupied, listings matching the address, utilities far above the pattern for the unit size.</p>"
      "<p>The agent's only action is to flag it to the property manager with the evidence, and to take no action that alerts the occupant.</p>"
      "<p>Accusing a tenant wrongly is worse than a month of unnoticed subletting. See [[Escalation Policy]] and [[Lease Clause Library]].</p>"]}]}]

 ;; Conversations that already happened. `:me` is the workspace owner; `:agent`
 ;; resolves to the resident agent, which simmis mints per room, so it cannot be
 ;; named by handle here.
 ;;
 ;; CONTENT FORMAT DIFFERS BY SPEAKER, because the renderer does: chat.cljc
 ;; renders agent messages as MARKDOWN and human messages as rich text. Human
 ;; lines are therefore plain, agent lines markdown — HTML in a human line shows
 ;; up as literal tags on screen.
 ;;
 ;; Both threads are written so the WIKI is load-bearing: the answers live in
 ;; the handbook, and the conversation shows someone reaching them. A demo
 ;; where the chat is self-contained proves nothing about the substrate.
 :rooms
 [{:name "Ops — Maintenance"
   ;; A real member of the room, minted through the same create-agent! +
   ;; add-party! path the "add agent" button uses — not just a name in the
   ;; transcript. Without this a room's only party is its owner, and asking
   ;; anything gets no reply because nothing is in there to answer.
   :agent {:name "Vár"
           :system-prompt "You are Vár, the operations secretary for Tröskel, a Swedish letting-agency SaaS. You handle tenant maintenance reports for the properties the team manages. You know the handbook — escalation policy, spend authority, the repair priority matrix, the contractor panel — and you follow it. When a change to the handbook is warranted, file a proposal rather than editing it directly. Money moves through the room's book. Be brief and concrete: a property manager reading you at 7am wants the decision, not the reasoning."}
   :kind :project
   :attach-kbs ["Tröskel Handbook" "Operations Playbook"]
   :attach-drives ["Legal & Compliance" "Finance"]
   :messages
   [{:from :me :at "2026-07-14T08:12:00Z"
     :text "Tenant at Lindholmen 12B says the radiators are cold but the hot water is fine. Outside it's about 3°C. What tier is that?"}
    {:from :agent :at "2026-07-14T08:12:40Z"
     :text "Hot water present with no heating usually points at the thermostat or a diverter valve, which *Tenant Reports No Heat* puts at P2 — **except** below 5°C outside, and you're at 3°C. So this is P1, same day.\n\nVärmepartner AB is the panel contractor for total heat loss with an 8-hour SLA. That's inside the 2 000 SEK band in *Spend Authority*, so I can book it without approval. Shall I, and offer temporary heaters?"}
    {:from :me :at "2026-07-14T08:15:10Z"
     :text "Yes, book it. Also — same flat had a gas smell reported back in March, does that change anything?"}
    {:from :agent :at "2026-07-14T08:16:02Z"
     :text "It does, but not for the tier. The incident log has Lindholmen 12B twice: 14 Jan (no leak found) and 22 Mar (loose fitting on the hob, corrected). Two gas reports at one property inside a quarter is the pattern *Incident Log* warns about — repeated false alarms usually mean something nobody has found yet.\n\nBooked the heating call-out. I'd also flag the gas history to the landlord and ask for an early safety check rather than waiting for the annual date. That's your call, not mine — it's a landlord cost."}
    {:from :me :at "2026-07-14T08:19:33Z"
     :text "Agreed, flag it. Good catch."}]}

  {:name "Product — Triage Quality"
   :agent {:name "Vár"
           :system-prompt "You are Vár, working with Tröskel's product team on how well the maintenance agents triage incoming reports. Look for patterns across incidents rather than handling them one at a time."}
   :kind :project
   :attach-kbs ["Tröskel Handbook"]
   :messages
   [{:from :me :at "2026-07-20T10:02:00Z"
     :text "Autonomous resolution on P2 is sitting at 61%. I want it higher before we talk to investors. Where's the loss?"}
    {:from :agent :at "2026-07-20T10:03:15Z"
     :text "Careful what we optimise. *Product Principles* says a predictable agent that escalates too often beats a clever one that surprises — so the number going up is only good if the escalations we remove were unnecessary ones.\n\nLooking at the escalation reasons, the largest bucket isn't judgement at all: it's cases where the lease and the handbook disagree, which *Escalation Policy* mandates escalating. Those are correct escalations and shouldn't be removed."}
    {:from :me :at "2026-07-20T10:06:40Z"
     :text "So what would you change?"}
    {:from :agent :at "2026-07-20T10:08:20Z"
     :text "Two things, neither of them the model.\n\nFirst, *Repair Priority Matrix* doesn't cover appliances the tenant owns versus the landlord's — that distinction is in *Lease Clause Library* but not in the triage table, so every case round-trips. Writing it into the matrix converts a class of escalation into a decision.\n\nSecond, the 2 000 SEK band in *Spend Authority* was set a year ago. A fair number of P2 jobs now quote just over it and escalate purely on price, not on risk.\n\nBoth are policy changes, not model changes. I can open a proposal for the first one — it's a handbook edit and you'd review the diff before it lands."}
    {:from :me :at "2026-07-20T10:11:05Z"
     :text "Do the first. Leave the spend threshold, that one needs the founders."}]}]

