   [{:path "2026/q1-summary.md"
     :content "# Tröskel — Q1 2026\n\nARR 2 480 000 SEK, up 22% QoQ.\nUnits under management: 41 200 across 19 agencies.\nNet revenue retention: 114%.\n\n## Notes\n\nGrowth is almost entirely expansion within existing agencies rather than new\nlogos. Two agencies moved Core -> Full after the deposit-preparation feature\nshipped, which is the clearest signal so far that the compliance work is what\nthey actually pay for.\n\nCAC payback sits at 11 months, which is acceptable at this stage but is\nflattered by two unusually large agencies onboarded in January.\n"}
    {:path "2026/pricing-model.md"
     :content "# Pricing model\n\nPer unit per month, billed monthly. See the Pricing page in the handbook.\n\n| tier | SEK/unit/mo | includes                                   |\n|------|-------------|--------------------------------------------|\n| Core | 29          | triage, messaging, compliance calendar      |\n| Full | 49          | + rent reconciliation, deposits, reporting  |\n\nFloor of 6 000 SEK/month below 200 units.\n\n## Why not per action\n\nCharging per agent action rewards a chatty agent. Our whole quality argument\nis that a good agent escalates rather than improvises, and we should not price\nagainst our own principle.\n"}
    {:path "2026/runway.md"
     :content "# Runway\n\nCash 14 200 000 SEK. Net burn 780 000 SEK/month. 18 months at current burn.\n\nTwo hires planned (one backend, one customer success) taking burn to roughly\n980 000 SEK/month from Q3, giving about 13 months from the end of Q2.\n\nWe do not intend to raise before demonstrating that autonomous P2 resolution\nholds above 60% across more than three agencies.\n"}]}

  {:name "Legal & Compliance"
   :files
   [{:path "templates/tenancy-agreement.md"
     :content "# Standard tenancy agreement (template)\n\nTemplate only. The executed agreement for any given tenancy governs, and where\nit differs from this template the executed version wins.\n\n## 4. Repairs\n\nThe landlord shall keep in repair the structure and exterior of the property,\nand keep in repair and proper working order the installations for the supply of\nwater, gas, electricity, sanitation, space heating and heating water.\n\n## 7. Access\n\nThe landlord may enter on 24 hours' written notice at reasonable times, save in\nan emergency where entry may be immediate.\n\n## 11. Subletting\n\nThe tenant shall not sublet or part with possession of the whole or any part of\nthe property without the landlord's prior written consent.\n"}
    {:path "templates/check-out-report.md"
     :content "# Check-out report (template)\n\nComplete within 48 hours of the tenancy end date, with the property empty.\n\nFor each room record: floor, walls, ceiling, windows, fixtures, cleanliness.\nPhotograph at the SAME angles as the move-in inventory — mismatched angles are\nthe most common reason a deduction fails at dispute.\n\nMeter readings: electricity, gas, water.\nKeys returned: count and type.\nForwarding address:\n\n## Fair wear and tear\n\nAge and expected life of the item must be stated for any deduction. A carpet at\nseven years of a ten-year expected life cannot be charged at replacement cost.\n"}
    {:path "compliance/gas-safety-schedule.md"
     :content "# Gas safety — annual schedule\n\nEvery property with a gas appliance requires an annual safety check by a\nregistered engineer, and a record supplied to the tenant within 28 days.\n\nA lapsed certificate is not a paperwork problem. It invalidates parts of the\nlandlord's position and, in the worst case, is the difference between an\nincident and a fatality.\n\nThe compliance calendar drives this. No property should ever reach the\nexpiry date without a booked appointment.\n"}
    {:path "compliance/incident-log.md"
     :content "# Incident log\n\nEvery gas or electrical incident is recorded here regardless of outcome,\nincluding false alarms. False alarms are useful: a property that generates\nrepeated false gas alarms usually has a real problem nobody has found.\n\n| date       | property        | type       | outcome                        |\n|------------|-----------------|------------|--------------------------------|\n| 2026-01-14 | Lindholmen 12B  | gas smell  | no leak found, tenant reassured |\n| 2026-02-03 | Majorna 4A      | burning    | failed socket replaced, EICR ok |\n| 2026-03-22 | Lindholmen 12B  | gas smell  | loose fitting on hob, corrected |\n"}]}]

 ;; A proposal already waiting for review, so the review surface has something
 ;; real in it on a fresh seed rather than an empty inbox.
 ;;
 ;; It ADDS a page, EDITS a clause, and RETRACTS one. The retraction is the
 ;; point: an all-additions proposal never demonstrates that withdrawing
 ;; something propagates through review the way adding it does, and withdrawal
 ;; is the harder half — it is what makes a wiki maintainable rather than
 ;; merely append-only.
 :proposals
 [{:title "Tighten escalation for repeat cold-heat reports"
   :summary "Adds a heating-specific escalation page, raises the repeat window
             from 30 to 60 days, and retires the clause that told agents to
             treat escalation as never being a failure — it was read as
             permission to escalate everything."
   :kb "Tröskel Handbook"
   :room "Ops — Maintenance"
   :new-pages
   [{:title "Heating Escalation"
     :at "2026-03-28T08:30:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>Heat is not like other repairs: a cold flat in a Gothenburg winter is a habitability question, not a comfort one, so the clock starts colder and runs faster.</p>"
      "<p>Below 18 °C measured in the living room, treat as P1 in [[Repair Priority Matrix]] regardless of the tenant's tone. Do not wait for the tenant to escalate — most will not.</p>"
      "<p>Book a panel contractor from [[Contractor Panel]] the same day, within the P1 limit in [[Spend Authority]]. If none is available, escalate rather than schedule for the next day.</p>"
      "<p>A second cold-heat report on the same unit within 60 days goes to a human immediately, per [[Escalation Policy]]. The first fix failed and the second call is not a repeat of the first — it is evidence.</p>"]}]
   :edits
   [{:page "Escalation Policy"
     :at "2026-03-28T08:40:00Z"
     ;; the 30-day repeat window was too short for heating, which is seasonal
     :edit-block {:index 4
                  :html "<p>The same issue has been reported twice in 60 days. A repeat is a signal the first fix failed — and for heating, a 30-day window missed the pattern entirely, because a cold snap and its recurrence are rarely a month apart.</p>"}
     ;; retires the clause that made over-escalation costless
     :remove-block 5}]}]

